---
/* L04 - probability and confidence are two different claims, and the
   overconfidence gap is measurable. One writer: the figures workstream E. */
---

<figure class="fig">
  <div class="fig__frame">
    <img
      class="fig__picture"
      src={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}illustrations/L04.jpg`}
      alt="A line drawing of two open notebooks lying side by side on a desk, the left page filled with a column of tick marks that a hand with a pen is adding to, the right page holding one line of writing that a finger points at."
    />
    <svg
      class="fig__svg"
      viewBox="0 0 640 340"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="L04-fig-title L04-fig-desc"
    >
      <title id="L04-fig-title">Two numbers, two claims, and the gap between them</title>
      <desc id="L04-fig-desc">
        On the left, two horizontal scales from zero to one. The upper one is labelled probability,
        about the world, and carries a marker at 0.3, which sits below a dashed threshold line at
        0.6. The lower one is labelled confidence, about the model, and carries a marker at 0.9,
        which sits above the same threshold, so a rule that reads confidence acts on a case the
        probability would not. On the right, a bar chart of how often the model was right out of one
        hundred. The first bar is labelled claimed 0.9 and seventy of one hundred right: a dashed
        line marks the claimed level and a bracket marks the gap of 0.2 between the claim and the
        reality. The second bar is labelled claimed 0.6 and sixty of one hundred right, and its
        solid bar meets its dashed claimed line exactly. A note says the expected calibration error
        is 0.0 when perfect and bigger when worse.
      </desc>

      <!-- the two numbers -->
      <text x="24" y="30" class="fig-head">one answer, two claims</text>

      <text x="24" y="72" class="fig-text">probability</text>
      <text x="24" y="88" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">the world</text>
      <line x1="120" y1="80" x2="400" y2="80" stroke="var(--color-rule)" stroke-width="1" />
      <circle cx="204" cy="80" r="5" fill="var(--color-ink)" />
      <text x="204" y="66" class="fig-num" text-anchor="middle">0.3</text>

      <text x="24" y="120" class="fig-text">confidence</text>
      <text x="24" y="136" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">the model</text>
      <line x1="120" y1="128" x2="400" y2="128" stroke="var(--color-rule)" stroke-width="1" />
      <g class="fig-travel" style="--m-travel: 252px; --m-duration: 1.2s;">
        <circle cx="372" cy="128" r="5" fill="var(--color-prussian)" />
      </g>
      <text class="m-fade fig-num fig-num--prussian" style="--m-duration: 0.6s; --m-delay: 1.2s;" x="372" y="112" text-anchor="middle">0.9</text>

      <!-- the one threshold, drawn once across both scales -->
      <line x1="288" y1="56" x2="288" y2="136" stroke="var(--color-prussian)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="5 4" />
      <text x="296" y="64" class="fig-num fig-num--prussian">threshold 0.6</text>
      <line x1="120" y1="144" x2="120" y2="150" stroke="var(--color-rule)" stroke-width="1" />
      <line x1="400" y1="144" x2="400" y2="150" stroke="var(--color-rule)" stroke-width="1" />
      <text x="120" y="164" class="fig-num">0</text>
      <text x="400" y="164" class="fig-num" text-anchor="end">1</text>

      <!-- what the wrong notebook costs -->
      <rect x="24" y="182" width="392" height="132" fill="none" stroke="var(--color-rule-soft)" stroke-width="1" />
      <text x="38" y="204" class="fig-text fig-text--ink">the rule reads the probability</text>
      <text x="38" y="226" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">read the probability and a 0.3 case stays</text>
      <text x="38" y="242" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">below the bar: the system gets cautious</text>
      <text x="38" y="262" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">read the confidence and the 0.9 crosses</text>
      <text x="38" y="278" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">any bar: the system acts hardest exactly</text>
      <text x="38" y="294" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">when it should have stayed quiet</text>

      <!-- the overconfidence gap -->
      <text x="440" y="30" class="fig-head">how often it was right</text>
      <line x1="456" y1="248" x2="616" y2="248" stroke="var(--color-rule)" stroke-width="1" />
      <line x1="456" y1="60" x2="456" y2="248" stroke="var(--color-rule)" stroke-width="1" />
      <text x="450" y="252" class="fig-num" text-anchor="end">0</text>
      <text x="450" y="64" class="fig-num" text-anchor="end">100</text>

      <rect x="474" y="116.4" width="46" height="131.6" fill="var(--color-prussian)" />
      <line x1="468" y1="78.8" x2="526" y2="78.8" stroke="var(--color-ink-45)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />
      <text x="497" y="72" class="fig-num" text-anchor="middle">0.9</text>
      <line x1="530" y1="78.8" x2="530" y2="116.4" stroke="var(--color-ink)" stroke-width="1" />
      <line x1="525" y1="78.8" x2="535" y2="78.8" stroke="var(--color-ink)" stroke-width="1" />
      <line x1="525" y1="116.4" x2="535" y2="116.4" stroke="var(--color-ink)" stroke-width="1" />
      <text x="538" y="101" class="fig-num fig-num--ink">0.2</text>
      <text x="497" y="262" class="fig-text" text-anchor="middle">70 of 100</text>
      <text x="497" y="276" class="fig-text fig-text--dim" text-anchor="middle">right</text>

      <rect x="548" y="135.2" width="46" height="112.8" fill="var(--color-prussian-tint)" />
      <line x1="542" y1="135.2" x2="600" y2="135.2" stroke="var(--color-ink-45)" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4 3" />
      <text x="571" y="128" class="fig-num" text-anchor="middle">0.6</text>
      <text x="571" y="262" class="fig-text" text-anchor="middle">60 of 100</text>
      <text x="571" y="276" class="fig-text fig-text--dim" text-anchor="middle">right</text>

      <text x="440" y="292" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">dashed = what it claimed,</text>
      <text x="440" y="305" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">solid = how often right</text>
      <text x="440" y="320" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">ECE 0.0 is perfect,</text>
      <text x="440" y="333" class="fig-text fig-text--dim">bigger is worse</text>
    </svg>
  </div>
  <figcaption class="fig__caption">
    <span class="edge-code">figure L04</span>
    <span class="fig__caption-text">The probability claims a rate in the world and the confidence claims a feeling in the model, so thresholding on the wrong one turns a shaky 0.3 answer into an action the world never justified.</span>
  </figcaption>
</figure>

<style>
  .fig__svg {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .fig-head {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.09em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    fill: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .fig-text {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    fill: var(--color-ink-70);
  }

  .fig-text--ink {
    fill: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .fig-text--dim {
    fill: var(--color-ink-45);
  }

  .fig-num {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    fill: var(--color-ink-70);
  }

  .fig-num--ink {
    fill: var(--color-ink);
  }

  .fig-num--prussian {
    fill: var(--color-prussian);
  }

  /* the one idea of motion: the confidence marker slides across its own scale
     and ends up on the far side of the threshold the probability never reached */
  @keyframes l04-travel {
    from {
      transform: translateX(calc(var(--m-travel, 252px) * -1));
    }
    to {
      transform: translateX(0);
    }
  }

  .fig-travel {
    animation: l04-travel var(--m-duration, 1.2s) ease-out both;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .fig-travel {
      animation: none !important;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
